WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.274
NIST's post-quantum cryptography effort used a multi-year public process to evaluate candidate algorithms.

00:00:05.274 --> 00:00:08.855
That process included security analysis, performance review, implementation considerations,

00:00:08.855 --> 00:00:10.447
and public cryptographic review.

00:00:10.447 --> 00:00:14.029
The open process matters because cryptographic confidence grows slowly.

00:00:14.029 --> 00:00:17.611
Algorithms need to survive review, attempted attacks, implementation experiments,

00:00:17.611 --> 00:00:22.387
and scrutiny from different communities before they are suitable for broad reliance.

00:00:22.387 --> 00:00:25.968
Security review is only one part of the evaluation.

00:00:25.968 --> 00:00:29.550
Algorithms also need acceptable performance, implementability, clear parameter choices,

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.734
and behavior that can fit into real protocols.

00:00:32.734 --> 00:00:37.510
A strong algorithm on paper can still create deployment problems if keys

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:39.897
are too large for constrained environments,

00:00:39.897 --> 00:00:43.081
signatures are too slow for common use cases,

00:00:43.081 --> 00:00:47.857
or implementations are difficult to make correct and resistant to side channels.

00:00:47.857 --> 00:00:51.041
The words selected and standardized are not interchangeable.

00:00:51.041 --> 00:00:55.816
A selected algorithm has been chosen for a path toward a standard,

00:00:55.816 --> 00:00:58.204
but the final document, naming, parameters,

00:00:58.204 --> 00:01:01.388
and implementation guidance may still be in progress.

00:01:01.388 --> 00:01:07.358
A standardized algorithm has a published standard that organizations and vendors can reference more directly.

00:01:07.358 --> 00:01:13.327
Migration programs should track both statuses so planning does not freeze at a single announcement.

00:01:13.327 --> 00:01:16.113
Organizations should also expect guidance to evolve.

00:01:16.113 --> 00:01:19.297
Standards, drafts, validation programs, protocol profiles, product support,

00:01:19.297 --> 00:01:21.685
and compliance expectations mature over time.

00:01:21.685 --> 00:01:27.256
A good PQC program watches the standards process, but it does not wait passively.

00:01:27.256 --> 00:01:30.838
It builds inventory, tests dependencies, asks vendors concrete questions,

00:01:30.838 --> 00:01:34.420
and designs cryptographic agility so future changes are manageable.
